= Oregano

_Scope: This entry treats the_ oreganum complex _of Mediterranean Lamiaceae —_ Origanum vulgare _L. (common oregano, Greek and Italian register),_ O. onites _L. (Turkish / Cretan oregano),_ O. syriacum _(L.) Siebold (Syrian / Lebanese oregano, the principal_ zaʿtar _of Levantine cuisine and the modern Israeli botanical identification of biblical_ ezov _"hyssop"), and the closely related and frequently confused_ marjoram _(_O. majorana_ L.). The entry treats_ Origanum vulgare _as the principal subject, with explicit cross-species notes on_ O. syriacum _(_zaʿtar baʿli_) and_ O. majorana _(_marwa_ / _marubaka_) given their distinct classical receptions. Status: deeply classical in Greco-Roman (Hippocrates, Dioscorides II.157 ὀρίγανον, Pliny, Galen), well-developed in Tibb (Levantine_ zaʿtar _and the medicinal_ ṣaʿtar farsi _register), with substantial Biblical attestation (the_ ezov _/ hyssop identification of Exodus 12:22 and Psalm 51 — modern botanical consensus identifies biblical ezov as O. syriacum, not_ Hyssopus officinalis_)._ Not _classical in Chinese (Mediterranean herb, not in Bencao tradition);_ not _classical in Ayurveda (the Indian classical_ marubaka _/_ marwa _refers to_ O. majorana_, not_ O. vulgare_, and even that with some commentarial uncertainty)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Origanum vulgare_ L., family Lamiaceae (Labiatae); the drug is the *dried aerial parts* — leaves, flowering tops, and inflorescences (_Origani herba_). Multiple species are pharmacopoeially and culinarily relevant: *_O. vulgare_* L. (common oregano, with the high-essential-oil subsp. _hirtum_ being the principal commercial "Greek oregano"); *_O. onites_* L. (Turkish / Cretan oregano); *_O. heracleoticum_* L. (Greek winter oregano); *_O. syriacum_* (L.) Siebold (Syrian / Lebanese oregano, _Lebanese zaʿtar_); *_O. majorana_* L. (sweet marjoram — botanically separated by some authorities as _Majorana hortensis_, but in modern Apiaceae-style taxonomy treated as Origanum). Latin _origanum_ from Greek ὀρίγανον (_oríganon_) — *etymology debated*: the folk etymology connects ὄρος (_óros_, "mountain") + γάνος (_gános_, "joy, brightness") for "mountain joy / mountain brightness" — fitting the high-altitude Mediterranean habitat — but the *true etymology is unsettled* and the word is probably a *pre-Greek substrate borrowing* with the folk etymology being secondary. Beekes treats the substrate-borrowing reading as the more likely.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short (s.v. _origanum_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. ὀρίγανον) — flags the word as probably a pre-Greek substrate borrowing, with the "mountain joy" reading as folk etymology; cf. discussion in Chantraine, _Dictionnaire étymologique de la langue grecque_. The folk etymology persists in modern popular and herbal literature; honest scholarly register flags it as folk.]

*English:* _Oregano_ — from Spanish _orégano_, from Latin _origanum_, from Greek ὀρίγανον. The Modern English form is a 20th-century borrowing via Spanish (largely from Mexican-American culinary register from the 1940s onward); older English usage was _wild marjoram_ for _O. vulgare_, with the Spanish form replacing this only in the modern era. The historical English literature uses "wild marjoram" / "marjoram" interchangeably with "oregano" — leading to substantial historical confusion in English-language herbal texts. *Marjoram* — from Old French _majorane_, from medieval Latin _maiorana_, of contested origin (folk-etymologized from _major_ but probably substrate); refers properly to _O. majorana_, the sweeter, milder, smaller-leaved cultivated species.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "oregano, n.", "marjoram, n."). The 20th-century English oregano-vs-marjoram disentanglement is recent; older English herbal literature (Gerard, Culpeper) uses these names interchangeably or in regionally-inconsistent ways — historical readers should beware.]

*Arabic / Levantine — the zaʿtar register:* the Levantine Arabic *زَعتَر (zaʿtar)* refers to a *category of related Lamiaceae* rather than a single species — including _Origanum syriacum_ (the principal *zaʿtar baʿli*, "dryland zaʿtar," wild-collected on Levantine hillsides), _Thymus serpyllum_ and related Thymus species (which English calls "thyme"), and *_Satureja_* (savory) species. Modern Levantine culinary and pharmaceutical usage treats *zaʿtar baʿli* (O. syriacum) as the canonical referent. The famous *zaʿtar spice mix* (the Palestinian, Lebanese, Syrian, and Jordanian dry-blend served with bread and olive oil) is *dried zaʿtar herb + sumac + toasted sesame + salt + sometimes other aromatics* — the canonical Levantine condiment, eaten daily across the Levantine Arab world and the modern Israeli kitchen. *Persian: مَرزَنجوش (marzanjūsh)* — for marjoram (_O. majorana_) and oregano-class species; the canonical Tibb name. *_Ṣaʿtar fārsī_* (Persian-style ṣaʿtar) = oregano in classical Tibb sometimes. The Arabic / Tibb nomenclature requires care: the same Arabic word can refer to closely-related but distinct species, and the classical Tibb texts do not always distinguish them clearly.#footnote[Standard Arabic-Levantine ethnobotany: Crowfoot & Baldensperger, _From Cedar to Hyssop_ (1932); modern Israeli botany; Daniel Zohary's classical-Mediterranean botany; standard regional culinary literature. The zaʿtar category in Levantine usage is essentially that of "wild Mediterranean Lamiaceae used as a Lebanese/Palestinian/Syrian dry herb"; the canonical species is _O. syriacum_, with Thymus species recognized as related.]

*Hebrew (Biblical):* *אֵזוֹב (ʾēzōv, hyssop)* — the canonical biblical name. The *modern Israeli botanical and rabbinic consensus identifies biblical ezov with _Origanum syriacum_* (Syrian / Lebanese oregano, the wild Levantine zaʿtar baʿli), *not* with the European _Hyssopus officinalis_ which the King James and other early English translations rendered as "hyssop." The biblical ezov appears in extraordinarily significant contexts:
- *Exodus 12:22* — the Passover blood-marking on the doorposts using a bundle of ezov (the founding ritual of the Exodus and the historical-religious identity of Israel).
- *Leviticus 14:4–6, 49–52* — purification of leprosy (_metsoraʿ_) and of houses with ezov, cedar wood, scarlet, and bird's blood.
- *Numbers 19:6, 18* — the Red Heifer purification ritual; ezov bundle for sprinkling.
- *1 Kings 4:33* — Solomon's catalog of plants, from "the cedar that is in Lebanon to the hyssop that springs out of the wall" — the great-and-small biblical merism.
- *Psalm 51:7* — *"Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean; wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow"* (David's penitential prayer) — the most famous liturgical use; preserved in the Christian penitential liturgy continuously.
- *John 19:29* — a sponge of vinegar on a stalk of hyssop offered to Jesus at the crucifixion (paralleling and reframing the Passover symbolism).
The ezov of biblical text is *almost certainly _O. syriacum_*, given the species' wild presence on Levantine walls and hillsides (the 1 Kings "hyssop on the wall" image is exactly right for O. syriacum), its small-and-bundleable structure, and the modern Israeli botanical-rabbinic consensus. *_Hyssopus officinalis_ (European hyssop)* is *not native to the Levant* and is the wrong plant for the biblical context.#footnote[The biblical ezov / hyssop identification with _Origanum syriacum_ is the modern Israeli botanical consensus, articulated in Zohary, _Plants of the Bible_ (Cambridge, 1982); Ehud Spanier, modern Israeli ethnobotany; and the rabbinic tradition (Maimonides, _Mishneh Torah_, _Hilkhot Para Aduma_ 11:7; the Mishnah _Niddah_ 6:11; _Tosefta Sukkah_ 2:7). The King James / older English "hyssop" translation as _Hyssopus officinalis_ is a misidentification preserved by translation tradition; the biblical plant is the Levantine wild oregano. The Passover blood-marking with ezov / O. syriacum is the founding ritual of Exodus 12:22 — making O. syriacum one of the most religiously significant plants in the biblical text.]

*Chinese:* *Origanum vulgare* and the European oregano-and-marjoram complex are *not classical in Chinese medicine*. The Mediterranean Lamiaceae are outside the Bencao tradition's geographical range. Modern Chinese: *牛至 (niúzhì)*, "ox-extreme" (a modern coinage; the etymology is opaque) — the standard modern Chinese name for _Origanum vulgare_ in modern Chinese phytotherapy and culinary translation. *披薩草 (pīsà cǎo)*, "pizza herb" — a clearly modern colloquial name. No 本草綱目 entry under any classical name. The reading by extension on classical Chinese-medicine axes (warm, pungent-aromatic, Lung-Spleen) is straightforward but is not canonical.

*Sanskrit:* *O. vulgare* (the principal subject of this entry) is *not classical in Ayurveda*. The classical Sanskrit *मरुबक (marubaka)* / *मर्व (marva)* — sometimes translated as "marjoram" and identified by some commentators with _O. majorana_ — is treated by Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu and other nighaṇṭus, but the species identification is uncertain and may refer to multiple Lamiaceae including marjoram, sweet basil (_Ocimum_), or wild thyme. The *_O. vulgare_ proper* (European common oregano) is *post-classical* in Indian dietetics, introduced via European trade. Modern Indian and Hindi *अजवाइन-पत्ता (ajvāin-pattā)* or simply *ओरेगानो (oregano)* — modern coinages.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Origanum vulgare has no classical Chinese entry._ The reading below is *reasoned extension* on classical axes, *not* canonical 本草 — flagged.

*Property (by reasoned extension):* Warm (溫), close to slightly hot.

*Flavor:* Pungent (辛), with bitter (苦) and aromatic notes.

*Channels (by extension):* Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃) — the standard placement for warm-pungent-aromatic Lamiaceae.

*Actions (by extension):*
- *解表散寒 (jiě biǎo sàn hán)* — releases surface, scatters cold; for cold-pattern common cold and chills.
- *溫中 (wēn zhōng)* — warms the middle; for cold-pattern dyspepsia.
- *化痰 (huà tán)* — transforms phlegm; for cold-damp cough.
- *殺菌 (shā jūn)* — antimicrobial (modern register; carvacrol-thymol fraction).
- *Topical use* — for fungal skin conditions, dilute essential oil.

*Cautions (by extension):* yin-vacuity heat; pregnancy at high therapeutic doses; the bitter-aromatic-warming intensity is contraindicated in heat-pattern conditions.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical for_ Origanum vulgare. The Sanskrit *marubaka* / *marva* refers (where it refers to Origanum at all) to *_O. majorana_* (sweet marjoram), not the European common oregano; even that identification is commentarial-debated. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles to _O. vulgare_ *by extension*, in the same register as previous reasoned-only entries.

*Rasa (taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter) — the standard reading for a warming-aromatic Lamiaceae.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp / penetrating), Sugandhi (सुगन्धि, fragrant).

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) — strong; converges with the Tibb Hot mizāj and the Galenic warming reading.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* No prabhāva can be assigned classically (no classical entry). A modern reading would note the *carvacrol-mediated broad-spectrum antimicrobial* effect — phytopharmacologically striking and not predictable from the rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka. Flagged as modern interpretive.

*Doṣa effect:* Kapha-Vāta-śāmaka (pacifies Kapha and Vāta); aggravates Pitta in excess.

*Karma (by extension):*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles and supports digestion.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta downward.
- _Kāsahara_ — anti-cough.
- _Krimighna_ — anthelmintic / antimicrobial.
- _Tvacya_ — skin-benefiting (topical antimicrobial register).
- _Jvarahara_ — anti-fever (mild).
- _Used in (modern reasoned context):_ digestive complaints, cough-cold-bronchial conditions, topical fungal / bacterial conditions.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha entries — extensions of conditional rules)_

- *(S, extended) Conditional viruddha:* Pitta excess; the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile is inappropriate in hot-pattern conditions.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Pregnancy* — high-dose essential oil should be avoided; culinary use is generally safe.
  - *Anticoagulant interaction* — modest theoretical concern; carvacrol has antiplatelet activity in vitro.
  - *Hypoglycemia* — modest; combine cautiously with antidiabetic medications.
  - *Lamiaceae allergy* — possible cross-reactivity (mint family).
  - *Essential oil toxicity at high doses* — undiluted oregano essential oil can be mucosally irritant; dilute for topical use; oral concentrated EO use should be moderate.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

The Tibb tradition treats the oregano-marjoram-thyme complex extensively, with care needed about which species is meant. Avicenna treats *مَرزَنجوش (marzanjūsh)* — sweet marjoram (_O. majorana_) — in _al-Qānūn_ Book II as a major aromatic; *_zaʿtar_* in the broader Levantine sense (including _O. syriacum_) is canonical in Maghrebi-Andalusian and Levantine Tibb. The hyssop / ezov of the biblical-Hebrew tradition entered Islamic-medical thought as a sacred plant via Christian-Islamic-Jewish shared Levantine register. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* *زَعتَر (zaʿtar)* — the broad Levantine category including _O. syriacum_, related Thymus, and other wild Lamiaceae. *مَرزَنجوش (marzanjūsh)* — marjoram and oregano-class herbs; canonical Avicennan name. *صَعتَر (ṣaʿtar)* — alternate transliteration. The Tibb classical distinguishes _ṣaʿtar fārsī_ (Persian thyme), _ṣaʿtar maqdūnī_ (Macedonian = Mediterranean oregano-class), and other regional types.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 3rd degree* per Avicenna for marzanjūsh (a strong placement, the heating intensity well above fennel or anise); some sources read the dryness as 2nd degree. The classical Tibb places this herb group among the *strong warming aromatic* drugs.

*Afʿāl:*
- *Mufattiḥ* — deobstruent (hepatic, splenic, urinary).
- *Muḥallil* — resolvent (for cold-pattern hardenings).
- *Kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* — carminative.
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl* — diuretic.
- *Mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* — emmenagogue.
- *Munaqqī li-l-ṣadr* — chest-cleansing; for cold-pattern cough.
- *Muqawwī al-maʿida* — stomach-tonic.
- *Munawwim — bafḍ al-hamm* — sleep-aiding and mood-elevating (interestingly attributed to marzanjūsh by Avicenna).
- *Muḍādd li-l-sumūm* — anti-toxin; classical use as antidote-component.
- *Muḍādd al-ʿufunah* — anti-putrefactive; the antimicrobial register, which the modern carvacrol pharmacology validates with surprising consistency.

*Istemālāt:* cold-pattern dyspepsia; cough; chest congestion; menstrual disorders (cold pattern); urinary tract complaints; toothache and oral hygiene; skin and wound infections (external).

*Mawāneʿ:* hot-mizāj patients; pregnancy at therapeutic doses (uterine-stimulant); excessive use causes _ṣadāʿ_ (headache) and intensity-related effects.

*Muṣliḥ:* rose-water; sandalwood; quince syrup — to temper the heating intensity.

*Badal:* _ṣaʿtar fārsī_ (Persian thyme); _hāshā_ (savory); other Lamiaceae aromatics.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *Zaʿtar mix (the Levantine condiment)* — though not strictly a medical _murakkab_, the Levantine zaʿtar (dried herb + sumac + sesame + salt) is taken as a *daily digestive-and-tonic food-medicine*, eaten with bread and olive oil for breakfast across the Levantine Arab world. The combination is dietetically intentional: zaʿtar (warming aromatic) + sumac (cooling sour) + sesame (snigdha-cooling) + salt — a textbook balance.
- *Sharbat-i Marzanjūsh* — marjoram syrup, used in convalescence and for chest conditions.
- *Topical oils with oregano / marjoram* — for joint pain, headache (the classical _ṣudāʿ_ register), and skin conditions.
- *Maʿjūn preparations* — marzanjūsh as supporting aromatic in compound electuaries.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* The hadith on *zaʿtar* — narrated by various paths — is included in some Tibb al-Nabawī compendia but has *contested chain authenticity*. The most-cited narration includes the Prophet's recommendation of zaʿtar for general health; some narrations focus on the antimicrobial / wound-healing use. Honest scholarly register: *cultural attestation in Muslim folk medicine is strong; hadith authenticity for specific narrations is variable; the broader Tibb-Avicennan attestation is rock-solid*. The *biblical hyssop (ezov)* connection is recognized in Islamic theology as part of the shared Abrahamic tradition (the Qurʾān references biblical events including the Exodus, though it does not name ezov specifically).#footnote[Tibb: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II, _marzanjūsh_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _marzanjūsh_, _ṣaʿtar_, and related entries; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_. Tibb al-Nabawī: zaʿtar narrations in al-Suyūṭī, _al-Jāmiʿ al-Ṣaghīr_; Ibn al-Qayyim, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_; hadith chain authenticity for specific narrations is variable and should be verified against modern hadith-criticism. The biblical _ezov_ / _O. syriacum_ identification: Zohary, _Plants of the Bible_ (Cambridge, 1982).]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use _O. vulgare_; modern Chinese culinary and phytotherapeutic use is post-classical.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Sanskrit formulas use _O. vulgare_ proper. *Marubaka cūrṇa* and related preparations, if they refer to _O. majorana_, exist in classical compendia but are minor; modern Indian dietetic use of European oregano is post-classical (post-colonial, mostly mid-20th-century).

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

*The deepest and most continuous classical tradition for this herb.*

- *Greco-Roman:* *Hippocrates* uses oregano (ὀρίγανον) for digestive, respiratory, and gynecological conditions. *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ III.27, _origanon herakleotikon_, etc.) treats *multiple oregano species* — distinguishing _Herakleian origanon_ (probably _O. heracleoticum_, the strongest medicinal), _onites_ (Cretan, _O. onites_), and ordinary common oregano. Used for cough, digestive complaints, urinary disorders, women's reproductive medicine, and as a topical wound application. *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XX) gives extensive uses including as an aphrodisiac, digestive, and antidote. *Galen* classes oregano as hot in the 3rd degree, drying — converging with the later Tibb mizāj exactly.
- *Roman culinary use:* oregano was a canonical herb in Roman cookery (Apicius); it persists continuously in Italian, Greek, and Sicilian cuisine.
- *Medieval European pharmacy:* oregano persists through monastic medicine and Salernitan pharmacy as an aromatic, digestive, and antispasmodic; Hildegard (_Physica_) and the late-medieval herbalists treat it.
- *Modern Western herbal:* oregano essential oil has emerged as a major modern phytotherapy product — for upper respiratory tract infections, candidiasis, gastrointestinal infections (the carvacrol-thymol antimicrobial register); ESCOP and Commission E have monographs. The high-dose oregano-oil capsule register is a 20th–21st-century development.
- *Italian and Greek cuisine:* the canonical pizza-and-tomato herb of southern Italy; the Greek salata herb; central to Mediterranean cuisine generally; *the most globally familiar Mediterranean culinary herb*.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Hippocrates _passim_; Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.27, 28, 29 (multiple oregano species) — Beck translation; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XX (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_. Italian and Greek culinary register: standard regional gastronomy. ESCOP _Monographs_, _Origani herba_; Commission E monograph.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. The Tibb register treats *marzanjūsh* (Avicennan classical) and the broader Levantine *zaʿtar* (regional / folk-Tibb) extensively; the zaʿtar daily-condiment tradition is preserved continuously across the Levantine Arab world.

=== Jewish / Biblical (ezov / hyssop)

The biblical *ezov* (Hebrew אֵזוֹב), identified with _Origanum syriacum_ by modern Israeli botanical and rabbinic consensus, is a *ritually central* plant in classical Israelite religion. Its uses are *not pharmaceutical in the medical sense* but ritually purificatory:
- *Passover blood-marking (Exodus 12:22)* — bundle of ezov dipped in blood of paschal lamb; the founding ritual of the Exodus.
- *Leprosy purification (Leviticus 14)* — ezov in the seven-day purification of the metsoraʿ (leper) and of leprous houses.
- *Red Heifer purification (Numbers 19)* — ezov in the sprinkling-purification with the ashes of the red heifer.
- *Psalm 51:7* — penitential prayer of David, preserved as one of the great Christian penitential psalms.
- *Crucifixion narrative (John 19:29)* — the hyssop stalk with vinegar-sponge at the cross, reframing Passover symbolism.
- *Modern rabbinic tradition:* the ezov plant retains a category status in halakhic discussions of purification rituals; the practical application in the absence of the Temple is limited but the identification (Maimonides, _Mishneh Torah_; Mishnah _Niddah_ 6:11) is preserved.

This is one of the materia medica's rare cases where the substance's *religious-ritual register* dwarfs its *medical-pharmaceutical register* in classical attestation.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Origanum vulgare_ and the broader oregano-thyme-zaʿtar Lamiaceae complex are *canonical Mediterranean herbs* — adapted to *warm-dry summer, cool-wet winter* (Köppen Csa, Csb) conditions across the Mediterranean basin, the Levant, the Maghreb, and the Iberian peninsula. The herb is *wild-collected* in the Greek and Levantine highland tradition, with high-essential-oil populations developing in the hot-dry summer hillsides — the herb's *medicinal intensity is precisely a climatic adaptation* to summer dryness. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Warm-dry Mediterranean (the canonical home climate)

The Mediterranean basin proper; Greek, Italian, Spanish, southern French, Cretan, and Cypriot highland habitats; Pitta-Vāta pressure in summer, Vāta-Kapha in winter.

*Traditional preparation.* Dried wild oregano as the *canonical Mediterranean culinary herb* — used in tomato preparations, fish, lamb, vegetables, bread, and the Greek salad-and-olive-oil register. *Oregano-tea* in Greek and Cretan folk medicine as digestive and respiratory tonic. The *Italian pizzaiola* (tomato-oregano sauce) and Greek *salata horiatiki* (village salad with feta and oregano) are the most globally familiar culinary preservations.

*Cares.* The warming-aromatic profile is climatically apt for *cool months and cool-pattern conditions* in the Mediterranean cycle; heavy summer use in hot-Pitta patients should be moderated. The Mediterranean traditional cuisine pairs oregano with *cooling-sour adjuvants* (lemon, vinegar, fresh tomato in summer) — a constitutional pairing.

=== Levantine (Lebanese, Palestinian, Syrian, Jordanian) — the zaʿtar register

The Levantine eastern Mediterranean basin; Lebanon, Palestine, Syria, Jordan, Israel, and the Iraqi western fringe. *_Origanum syriacum_* (zaʿtar baʿli) is the dominant species. The constitutional climate is *warm-dry Mediterranean*, with strong *summer-Pitta* and *winter-cooling-damp* dynamics.

*Traditional preparation.* *The zaʿtar daily breakfast* — dried zaʿtar herb + sumac + toasted sesame + salt (the canonical four-component blend), eaten with flatbread (_khubz_) and olive oil for breakfast. This is the daily Levantine condiment-staple, preserved continuously across Lebanese, Palestinian, Syrian, Jordanian, and modern Israeli cuisines. *Olive-oil-and-zaʿtar* (_zaʿtar manaʾish_ — flatbread topped with olive-oil-and-zaʿtar paste, baked) is the canonical breakfast street food. *Wild-collected zaʿtar* (zaʿtar baʿli — "rainfed zaʿtar," wild-gathered on Levantine hillsides) is treated as superior to cultivated.

*Cares.* The Levantine breakfast pairing of zaʿtar + sumac + sesame + olive oil is *constitutionally calibrated*: warming-aromatic zaʿtar balanced by cooling-sour sumac, snigdha-cooling sesame, and snigdha olive oil. The composite is climatically apt for the warm-dry Mediterranean climate, summer or winter.

=== Continental Italian / Spanish (cooler Mediterranean) — secondary classical

Northern Italian, central Spanish, southern French — cooler-summer Mediterranean fringe.

*Traditional preparation.* Oregano in tomato and pasta preparations; Italian regional cuisines; Provençal herbs blends.

=== Climates where oregano is not traditional

Cold-damp Atlantic, hot-humid tropical, cold-dry high-altitude, polar — oregano is post-classical in these zones; modern Western culinary use is global but the climatic-constitutional reading is most coherent in the Mediterranean register.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

Modern globalized cuisine has spread oregano (and zaʿtar) across all climates; the high-dose oregano-essential-oil supplement register has further globalized the herb for antimicrobial use across constitutional types. The classical Mediterranean register — *warming-aromatic balanced with cooling-sour-snigdha adjuvants in compound condiment preparations* — gives a finer constitutional reading than the modern flat-essential-oil supplement use. Heavy daily essential-oil use in Pitta-loaded constitutions or hot-summer conditions can compound Pitta heat; the classical condiment register (whole-leaf, in compound preparations with balancing ingredients) is more climatically and constitutionally apt than the high-dose isolated-essential-oil register. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the warm-dry Mediterranean profile.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (dried leaf, per 100 g of typical commercial dried oregano):*
- Energy ~265 kcal; Water ~10 g
- Carbohydrate ~69 g; *fiber ~43 g* (very high — characteristic of dried herbs)
- Protein ~9 g; Fat ~4 g
- Minerals: *iron ~37 mg* (extremely high — dried oregano is one of the highest iron-content foods by weight), calcium ~1600 mg (very high), magnesium ~270 mg, potassium ~1260 mg, manganese very high
- Vitamins: vitamin K (very high — ~621 μg, blood-clotting-relevant), vitamin E, vitamin A, B-vitamins
- *Essential oil (3–7% of dried herb):* *carvacrol* (predominant, 40–80% depending on chemotype) and *thymol* (the two principal phenolic monoterpenes — the basis of most of the antimicrobial pharmacology); *p-cymene*, *γ-terpinene*, *linalool*, *terpinen-4-ol*, *β-caryophyllene*, *germacrene D*. The *carvacrol chemotype* is the principal commercial medicinal type; chemotypes vary by region (Greek high-carvacrol; Italian higher in linalool; Lebanese O. syriacum predominantly carvacrol).
- *Phenolic acids and flavonoids:* rosmarinic acid (the headline polyphenol — anti-inflammatory and antioxidant), apigenin, luteolin, quercetin, vicenin.
- *Triterpenes:* ursolic acid, oleanolic acid.#footnote[Phytochemistry: extensive modern pharmacognosy; standard references (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); ESCOP _Monographs_, _Origani herba_; recent reviews in _Phytotherapy Research_, _Phytomedicine_, _Industrial Crops and Products_. The chemotype distinction (carvacrol vs. thymol vs. linalool) is the principal practical chemical lever.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Broad-spectrum antimicrobial — the headline modern action*. Carvacrol and thymol have *robust in vitro and in vivo activity* against bacteria (Gram-positive and Gram-negative, including some MRSA strains), fungi (Candida species particularly), and some viruses and parasites. The mechanism is membrane-disruption — carvacrol intercalates into the lipid bilayer and permeabilizes microbial membranes. Clinical translation is *modest but real*: oregano essential oil and standardized extracts have demonstrated efficacy in some upper respiratory tract conditions, candidiasis, and gastrointestinal disorders, with several small RCTs supporting use.
- *Antioxidant* — high rosmarinic acid and polyphenol content; standard antioxidant register; modest clinical translation.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical and modest clinical; rosmarinic acid and carvacrol mechanisms.
- *Digestive carminative and antispasmodic* — the cross-system convergent classical action; preclinical and modest clinical support.
- *Hypoglycemic (mild)* — preclinical; modest clinical relevance.
- *Antiparasitic / anthelmintic* — preclinical activity; some clinical use against gastrointestinal parasites.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register (whole dried leaf in cooking, zaʿtar blend, etc.):* unrestricted at normal culinary intake.
- *Dried herb infusion / tea:* 1–3 g of dried herb per cup, 1–3 times daily.
- *Tincture:* 1–3 mL of 1:5 tincture, 2–3 times daily.
- *Essential oil — internal use (capsule / liquid):* *requires careful dosing* — typically 0.2–0.4 mL per day (ESCOP-influenced range); *concentrated essential oil is mucosally irritant and should not be taken neat*. Capsule preparations with emulsified or food-grade-diluted oil are the appropriate form.
- *Essential oil — topical:* dilute to 1–5% in carrier oil; undiluted oregano oil is *dermal-irritant* and can cause burns and contact dermatitis.
- *Pregnancy:* therapeutic doses (essential oil, high-dose supplement) should be *avoided*; culinary use is generally safe.
- *Anticoagulant interaction:* modest theoretical concern; carvacrol has antiplatelet activity in vitro.
- *Lamiaceae allergy:* cross-reactivity with other mint-family herbs; rare.
- *Concentrated EO toxicity:* high-dose internal use can cause gastrointestinal upset, mucosal irritation, possibly hepatotoxicity at very high doses (preclinical evidence).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* High-dose essential oil use should be under clinician supervision.#footnote[Dose ranges: ESCOP _Monographs_, _Origani herba_; Commission E; clinical literature on oregano essential oil. The high-dose oregano-oil capsule register is a relatively recent modern phytotherapy development and the long-term safety profile at chronic high-dose use is less well-characterized than the culinary register.]

